        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "432199",
          "text": "<p>Let R and S be two relations with the following schema</p><p>$R(\\underline{P,Q}, R1, R2, R3)$</p><p>$S(\\underline{P,Q}, S1, S2)$</p><p>where $\\left\\{P, Q\\right\\}$ is the key for both schemas. Which of the following queries are equivalent?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-roman\"><li><p>$\\Pi_P \\left(R \\bowtie S\\right)$</p></li><li><p>$\\Pi_P \\left(R\\right) \\bowtie \\Pi_P\\left(S\\right)$</p></li><li><p>$\\Pi_P \\left(\\Pi_{P, Q} \\left(R\\right) \\cap \\Pi_{P,Q} \\left(S\\right) \\right)$</p></li><li><p>$\\Pi_P \\left(\\Pi_{P, Q} \\left(R\\right) - \\left(\\Pi_{P,Q} \\left(R\\right) - \\Pi_{P,Q} \\left(S\\right)\\right)\\right)$</p></li></ol><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>Only I and II</li><li>Only I and III</li><li>Only I, II and III</li><li>Only I, III and IV</li></ol><p> </p>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "D",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses_csda_wq21,goclasses,databases,two-marks",
          "category": "Databases"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "432204",
          "text": "<p>With respect to relational algebra, which of the following operations are included from mathematical set theory?</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:lower-roman\" type=\"i\"><li>Join</li><li>Intersection</li><li>Cartisian product</li><li>Project<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>i and iv</li><li>ii and iii</li><li>iii and iv</li><li>ii and iv</li></ol></li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "B",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses_csda_wq21,goclasses,databases,one-mark",
          "category": "Databases"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "432196",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following instances:</p><p><br><img alt=\"\" width=\"301\" height=\"139\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"></p><p>Which of the following relational operations will produce the following tuple only?<br><img alt=\"\" width=\"220\" height=\"65\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"></p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>$($ $\\mathrm{QB}1\\cup \\mathrm{QB} 2) \\times(\\mathrm{QB} 2 \\cup \\mathrm{QB} 1)$</li><li>$(Q B 1-Q B 2) \\cap(Q B 2-Q B 1)$</li><li>$($ $\\mathrm{QB}1-\\mathrm{QB} 2) \\times(\\mathrm{QB} 2-\\mathrm{QB} 1)$</li><li>&nbsp;$(Q B 1 \\cap Q B 2)-(Q B 2 \\cap Q B 1)$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses_csda_wq21,goclasses,databases,two-marks",
          "category": "Databases"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "432198",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following relation schemas:<br>$R(A, B), \\; S(B, C), \\; T(C, D)$.<br>Check equivalences that are correct under set semantics.<br>Assume that all set operations ($\\cup, -$) are applied only on union-compatible relations (i.e., relations having the same set of attributes).<br> </p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>$ R \\bowtie R \\equiv R $</li><li>$ R \\cup (S - R) \\equiv S \\cup R $<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<em>(Assume $R$ and $S$ are union-compatible.)</em></li><li>$ (R \\cup S) - T \\equiv R \\cup (S - T) $<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<em>(Assume $R, S,$ and $T$ are union-compatible.)</em></li><li>$\\pi_{A}(R \\bowtie S) \\equiv \\pi_{A}(S \\bowtie R)$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "a;b;d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_csda_wq21,goclasses,databases,two-marks,multiple-selects",
          "category": "Databases"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "432200",
          "text": "<p>Consider the relations $R(A, B)$ and $S(B, C)$ and the following four relational algebra queries over R ans S</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-roman\"><li>$\\pi_{A, B} (R \\bowtie S)$</li><li>$R \\bowtie \\pi_B(S)$</li><li>$R \\cap (\\pi_A(R) \\times \\pi_B (S))$</li><li>$\\Pi_{A.&nbsp;R. B} (R \\times S)$ where $R.B$ refers to the column B in table R</li></ol><p>One can determine that:</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>I, III and IV are the same query</li><li>II, III and IV are the same query</li><li>I, II&nbsp;and IV are the same query</li><li>I, II and III&nbsp;are the same query</li></ol><p> </p>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "D",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses_csda_wq21,goclasses,databases,two-marks",
          "category": "Databases"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "432205",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following schemas:</p><p>Branch=(Branch-name, Assets, Branch-city)</p><p>Customer = (Customer-name, Bank name, Customer-city)</p><p>Borrow = (Branch-name, Loan number, Customer account-number)</p><p>Deposit = (Branch-name, Account-number, Customer-name, Balance)</p><p>Using relational Algebra, the Query that finds customers who have balance more than 10,000 is _____</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>$\\pi_{customer-name} (\\sigma_{balance &gt; 10000^{(Deposit)}}$)</li><li>$\\sigma_{customer-name} (\\sigma_{balance &gt; 10000^{(Deposit)}}$)</li><li>$\\pi_{customer-name} (\\sigma_{balance &gt; 10000^{(Borrow)}}$)</li><li>$\\sigma_{customer-name} (\\pi_{balance &gt; 10000^{(Borrow)}}$)</li></ol><p> </p>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "A",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses_csda_wq21,goclasses,databases,one-mark",
          "category": "Databases"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "432201",
          "text": "<p>The join operation can be defined as</p><ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>a cartesian product of two relations followed by a selection</li><li>a cartesian product of two relations</li><li>a union of two relations followed by cartesian product of the two relations</li><li>a union of two relations</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "A",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses_csda_wq21,goclasses,databases,one-mark",
          "category": "Databases"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "432197",
          "text": "<p>Suppose that two relations $R(A, B)$ and $S(A, B)$ have exactly the same schema. Consider the following equalities in relational algebra?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-roman\"><li>$\\quad R \\cap S=R-(R-S)$</li><li>$R \\cap S=S-(S-R)$</li><li>$R \\cap S=R$ NATURAL-JOIN $S$</li><li>$\\mathrm{R} \\cap \\mathrm{S}=\\mathrm{R} \\times \\mathrm{S}$</li></ol><p><br>Which of the equalities hold in relational algebra?</p><p> </p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>I only</li><li>I and II only</li><li>I, II and III</li><li>I, II, III and IV</li></ol><p> </p>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses_csda_wq21,goclasses,databases,two-marks",
          "category": "Databases"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "432202",
          "text": "<p>Which operation is used to extract specified columns from a table?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>Project</li><li>Join</li><li>Extract</li><li>Substitute</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "A",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses_csda_wq21,goclasses,databases,one-mark",
          "category": "Databases"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "432203",
          "text": "<p>Given the relations</p><ul><li>employee (name, salary, dept-no), and</li><li>department (dept-no, dept-name,address),</li></ul><p>Which of the following queries cannot be expressed using the basic relational algebra operations $\\left(\\sigma, \\pi,\\times ,\\Join, \\cup, \\cap,-\\right)$?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>Department address of every employee</li><li>Employees whose name is the same as their department name</li><li>The sum of all employees' salaries</li><li>All employees of a given department</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "C",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses_csda_wq21,goclasses,databases,one-mark",
          "category": "Databases"
        }
      ]
